### What is the Binary Tree Path Sum problem?

Given a binary tree and an integer target sum, determine whether the tree has any root-to-leaf path whose node values sum to exactly the target. Return true if such a path exists, false otherwise.

A root-to-leaf path starts at the root and ends at a leaf (a node with no children). Every node on the path contributes its value to the sum.

### Why is DFS the natural fit?

DFS naturally follows one root-to-leaf path at a time, which is exactly what we need: walk down a path, track the running sum, check at the leaf, and backtrack to try other paths. The recursion stack automatically handles the backtracking.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ApprovalBudgetChecker {

    static class ApprovalDesk {
        BigDecimal fee;
        ApprovalDesk left, right;
        ApprovalDesk(BigDecimal fee) { this.fee = fee; }
    }

    public static boolean hasPathSum(ApprovalDesk node, BigDecimal target) {
        if (node == null) return false;

        BigDecimal remaining = target.subtract(node.fee);

        // Leaf test: both children null
        if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {
            return remaining.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0;
        }

        // Recurse into children with logical OR
        return hasPathSum(node.left, remaining) || hasPathSum(node.right, remaining);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //       500
        //      /   \
        //    200   300
        //   /   \
        //  100   150
        ApprovalDesk root = new ApprovalDesk(new BigDecimal("500"));
        root.left = new ApprovalDesk(new BigDecimal("200"));
        root.right = new ApprovalDesk(new BigDecimal("300"));
        root.left.left = new ApprovalDesk(new BigDecimal("100"));
        root.left.right = new ApprovalDesk(new BigDecimal("150"));

        BigDecimal budget = new BigDecimal("800");
        System.out.println("Path sum " + budget + ": " + hasPathSum(root, budget));
        // Path: 500 -> 200 -> 100 = 800? Yes!

        BigDecimal budget2 = new BigDecimal("850");
        System.out.println("Path sum " + budget2 + ": " + hasPathSum(root, budget2));
        // Path: 500 -> 200 -> 150 = 850? Yes!

        BigDecimal budget3 = new BigDecimal("900");
        System.out.println("Path sum " + budget3 + ": " + hasPathSum(root, budget3));
        // No path sums to 900
    }
}
```

```text
Path sum 800: true
Path sum 850: true
Path sum 900: false
```

### Why must you check for a leaf, not for null?

The leaf test is the most important correctness detail. You must only declare success at a true leaf where both children are null, never at a null child. If you check at null, a path that merely passes through a node whose value equals the remaining target would falsely succeed before the path actually ends.

```java
// WRONG: checking at null
public static boolean hasPathSumBuggy(ApprovalDesk node, BigDecimal target) {
    if (node == null) return target.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0;
    // BUG: for a node with only a left child, the null right child
    // might return true if the remaining target happens to be 0,
    // even though the path hasn't reached a real leaf.
    return hasPathSumBuggy(node.left, target.subtract(node.fee))
        || hasPathSumBuggy(node.right, target.subtract(node.fee));
}

// CORRECT: checking at leaf
public static boolean hasPathSumCorrect(ApprovalDesk node, BigDecimal target) {
    if (node == null) return false;
    BigDecimal remaining = target.subtract(node.fee);
    if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {
        return remaining.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0;
    }
    return hasPathSumCorrect(node.left, remaining)
        || hasPathSumCorrect(node.right, remaining);
}
```

### How does the iterative DFS version work?

Use an explicit stack of pairs (node, remaining target) to simulate the recursion.

```java
import java.util.Stack;

public static boolean hasPathSumIterative(ApprovalDesk root, BigDecimal target) {
    if (root == null) return false;

    Stack<Object[]> stack = new Stack<>();
    stack.push(new Object[]{root, target.subtract(root.fee)});

    while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
        Object[] pair = stack.pop();
        ApprovalDesk node = (ApprovalDesk) pair[0];
        BigDecimal remaining = (BigDecimal) pair[1];

        if (node.left == null && node.right == null
                && remaining.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0) {
            return true;
        }
        if (node.right != null) {
            stack.push(new Object[]{node.right, remaining.subtract(node.right.fee)});
        }
        if (node.left != null) {
            stack.push(new Object[]{node.left, remaining.subtract(node.left.fee)});
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

### How is this related to other path sum variants?

| Variant | Description | Approach |
|---|---|---|
| Path Sum I (this) | Does any root-to-leaf path equal target? | DFS, return boolean |
| Path Sum II | Find all root-to-leaf paths equalling target | DFS + backtracking, collect paths |
| Path Sum III | Count paths starting at any node | Prefix sum + hashmap |
| Max Path Sum | Maximum sum path between any two nodes | DFS with global max |

```java
// Path Sum II: collect all valid paths
public static List<List<BigDecimal>> findAllPaths(
        ApprovalDesk node, BigDecimal target, List<BigDecimal> currentPath) {
    List<List<BigDecimal>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    if (node == null) return result;
    currentPath.add(node.fee);
    BigDecimal remaining = target.subtract(node.fee);
    if (node.left == null && node.right == null
            && remaining.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(currentPath));
    }
    result.addAll(findAllPaths(node.left, remaining, currentPath));
    result.addAll(findAllPaths(node.right, remaining, currentPath));
    currentPath.remove(currentPath.size() - 1); // backtrack
    return result;
}
```

### What about negative node values?

When node values can be negative, you cannot prune a branch early just because the remaining target went negative. A negative node deeper in the tree could bring the sum back to the target. You must always reach a leaf to decide.

```java
// With negative values: cannot prune early
//     5
//    / \
//   -3   2
//  /
// 4
// Target: 6. Path: 5 -> -3 -> 4 = 6. Remaining goes negative then positive.
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Null root** – return false.
- **Single node (leaf)** – return whether its value equals the target.
- **Target is zero with non-zero nodes** – must still walk to leaves; intermediate zeros do not count.
- **All negative values** – negative target might have a valid path.

```java
// Edge case: single node
ApprovalDesk single = new ApprovalDesk(new BigDecimal("42"));
System.out.println(hasPathSum(single, new BigDecimal("42"))); // true
System.out.println(hasPathSum(single, new BigDecimal("0")));  // false
```

### What are common mistakes?

Besides the null-vs-leaf bug, the most common mistake is using `==` instead of `.compareTo()` for BigDecimal comparison.

```java
// WRONG: == compares object references
if (remaining == BigDecimal.ZERO) // always false for new BigDecimal

// CORRECT: use compareTo
if (remaining.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0)
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n) because each node is visited at most once. Space is O(h) for the recursion stack, where h is the height of the tree. The logical OR short-circuits: once a valid path is found, the right subtree is not explored, but the worst case visits all nodes.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognise Path Sum as the simplest instance of the Tree Depth First Search pattern. The subtraction approach (passing `target - node.value` down) is cleaner than passing an accumulating sum, because it avoids a separate base-case comparison.

They should also know Path Sum III (count paths from any node) which requires prefix sums with a hashmap for O(n) time, and Max Path Sum which requires tracking global maximums through DFS. Understanding the full Path Sum family shows depth in tree DFS.
